<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Catalog>
    <!-- Debug only. Deliberately a buff rather than a change to the weapon:
         the player-DPS to worker-DPS ratio is the single number P0 exists to
         measure, so retuning the weapon to make testing comfortable would
         quietly destroy the thing being tested. This leaves the real numbers
         alone and can be switched on when the loop needs to be driven fast.

         Expires on its own, and carries an icon on the unit while it is up, so
         a session cannot be measured with it accidentally left running. -->
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Debug_Haste">
        <Alignment value="Positive"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-stimpack.dds"/>
        <Duration value="600"/>
        <Modification MoveSpeedMultiplier="2" AttackSpeedMultiplier="10"/>
    </CBehaviorBuff>

    <!-- The inner-room stockpile, as a real harvestable node.

         RemoveWhenEmpty is off, which is the whole reason this works: a stock
         mineral field deletes itself when it runs dry, and this one has to
         survive being emptied so clearing more debris can top it back up.
         Capacity is far above anything a session will reach, because the cap
         is not a design number, how fast debris fills it is.

         IdealHarvesterCount is display only, a suggestion shown in the UI; it
         does not change yield. Throughput is HarvestTime against HarvestAmount,
         and those are the two numbers to design. -->
    <CBehaviorResource id="Lob_Pile_Ore">
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\icon-mineral.dds"/>
        <Capacity value="100000"/>
        <Contents value="0"/>
        <HarvestTime value="2.786"/>
        <HarvestAmount value="5"/>
        <Flags index="RemoveWhenEmpty" value="0"/>
        <ReturnDelay value="0.5"/>
        <IdealHarvesterCount value="2"/>
    </CBehaviorResource>

    <!-- Recovery in the rest hall (baseline 8). Applied and removed by script
         according to where the character is and whether the core is still held,
         but the rate itself is data, so a welfare-department upgrade later
         changes a number here rather than any code. -->
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_RestHall_Recovery">
        <Alignment value="Positive"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-scannersweep.dds"/>
        <Modification>
            <VitalRegenArray index="Life" value="12"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Employee stats.

         Two behaviors per stat, because the engine splits the job in two and
         the split is not optional:

           CBehaviorAttribute  displays the stat and carries its per-point
                               effect. core's InfoPaneHero layout reserves
                               AttributeLabel1..5 down the right of the hero
                               pane to draw exactly these, named from
                               Behavior/Name and Behavior/Tooltip. MaxPoints
                               caps it. War3's STR/AGI/INT are this catalog.

           CBehaviorBuff       holds the points. An attribute's point total is
                               NOT its own stack count; points arrive only
                               through some other behavior's
                               Modification/AttributeChangeArray. So one
                               stackable hidden buff per stat, worth one point
                               per stack, and the stack count is the stat.

         That second half is the correction. Reading and writing the attribute
         behavior directly looked right and does nothing: UnitBehaviorCount on
         it answered 0 before and after, because there was no channel from the
         script to the points at all. Blizzard's own case is starcoop's
         AbathurPropArmor, fed by BiomassBuff1/10/100 carrying Points 1/10/100.

         MaxPoints and MaxStackCount are both 100 so EX is representable.
         Galaxy still clamps to 99, which is what keeps EX behind whatever ends
         up lifting it.

         Modification is empty on all four attributes. Fortitude is life,
         Justice is attack speed, Temperance is work speed, Prudence is SP; the
         first two have engine-side homes today and the other two do not exist
         yet, and a number invented here would be balance hidden in the last
         place anyone would look. When they land, they land here, per point, no
         script.

         Icons are all from the Void (Mod) dependency chain. Two of the
         originals were campaign-only assets, which the map cannot resolve.
         ============================================================ -->
    <CBehaviorAttribute id="Lob_Attr_Fortitude">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxPoints value="100"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-upgrade-terran-infantryarmorlevel1.dds"/>
    </CBehaviorAttribute>
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Point_Fortitude">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxStackCount value="100"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="Permanent" value="1"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="EnabledWhileDead" value="1"/>
        <InfoFlags index="Hidden" value="1"/>
        <Modification>
            <AttributeChangeArray Attribute="Lob_Attr_Fortitude" Points="1"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>
    <CBehaviorAttribute id="Lob_Attr_Prudence">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxPoints value="100"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-battlefieldawareness.dds"/>
    </CBehaviorAttribute>
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Point_Prudence">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxStackCount value="100"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="Permanent" value="1"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="EnabledWhileDead" value="1"/>
        <InfoFlags index="Hidden" value="1"/>
        <Modification>
            <AttributeChangeArray Attribute="Lob_Attr_Prudence" Points="1"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>
    <CBehaviorAttribute id="Lob_Attr_Temperance">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxPoints value="100"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-holdfire.dds"/>
    </CBehaviorAttribute>
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Point_Temperance">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxStackCount value="100"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="Permanent" value="1"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="EnabledWhileDead" value="1"/>
        <InfoFlags index="Hidden" value="1"/>
        <Modification>
            <AttributeChangeArray Attribute="Lob_Attr_Temperance" Points="1"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>
    <CBehaviorAttribute id="Lob_Attr_Justice">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxPoints value="100"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-upgrade-terran-infantryweaponslevel1.dds"/>
    </CBehaviorAttribute>
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Point_Justice">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <MaxStackCount value="100"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="Permanent" value="1"/>
        <BehaviorFlags index="EnabledWhileDead" value="1"/>
        <InfoFlags index="Hidden" value="1"/>
        <Modification>
            <AttributeChangeArray Attribute="Lob_Attr_Justice" Points="1"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>

    <!-- Employee rank, as the unit's one veterancy.

         Rank is the sum of the four stat levels, and the hero pane's level
         display is a sum over veterancy behaviors — so with exactly one
         veterancy on the unit, the native level readout IS the rank and the
         experience bar IS the progress toward the next one. That is what the
         five rank buffs were faking, and they are gone.

         XP is fed the sum of the four stat levels (4 at minimum, 24 at all-EX)
         by src/galaxy/11_employee.galaxy, and the rows below are the design's
         thresholds written straight out:

             I (sum 4-5)    II 6    III 9    IV 12    V 16

         MinVeterancyXP is the ABSOLUTE total for that level, not the increment
         to reach it. Measured, not assumed: with the rows written as increments
         (0 6 3 3 4) an employee at 6 XP came out level V, because 6 clears 6, 3,
         3 and 4 all at once. Stock data reads either way — Dehaka has 100 on
         every row, which is the same curve under both — so the flat repeats
         there are not evidence for increments, and War3's rising 100/150/200
         series is the shape an absolute table has anyway.

         Every XPFraction is zero: rank is derived, so nothing may feed it
         except the line that recomputes it. A kill that nudged this would put
         the panel out of step with Emp_Rank, which is the number every rule
         actually reads. -->
    <CBehaviorVeterancy id="Lob_Rank">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <Flags index="CombineXP" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="Kills" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageDealt" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageTaken" value="0"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="6"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="9"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="12"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="16"/>
    </CBehaviorVeterancy>
</Catalog>
